#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222315} :activity/id #uuid "081b50ee-0118-5b55-ada7-29231441c411" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048990 :topic/id #uuid "d1a9318f-8bb8-5f48-a251-a72b43bbe791" :topic/math-academy-id 6377} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222315 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222490 :tutorial/content "Recall that for $x'(t) = f(x),$ a point $x^\\ast$ is an *equilibrium* if $f(x^\\ast) = 0.$ Solutions starting at $x^\\ast$ remain constant for all $t.$\n\nNow, consider a system in the plane with variables $x(t)$ and $y(t){:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}(x,y) \\\\ {f}_{2}(x,y)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast = (x^\\ast, y^\\ast)$ is an **equilibrium point** if $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast) = \\mathbf{0}.$ Both components must be zero:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}^{∗},{y}^{∗})=0 \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}^{∗},{y}^{∗})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ then $\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ for all $t.$ The state does not change.\n\n**Note:** In the phase plane, $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ represents velocity. At an equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ the velocity is $\\mathbf{0},$ so trajectories starting there stay there forever.\n\nFor example, let's find the equilibrium points of the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}=x-y \\\\ {y}^{'}=x+y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** Set the right-hand sides equal to zero.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=0 \\\\ x+y-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:** Solve the system. From the first equation, $y = x.$ Substitute into the second:\n\n$$\nx + (x) - 2 = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 2x = 2 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x = 1.\n$$\n\nThus, $y = 1.$\n\nTherefore, the system has a single equilibrium point $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7af2084d-7c8b-577e-b656-d1040dddaabd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14487 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c2ee65c6-624f-52a8-8b0a-ed23e84593bc" :step/math-academy-id 588701 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222316 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221412 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221174 :question/id #uuid "c953f344-77dd-5eaa-9f57-33626a392041" :question/math-academy-id "e-21665"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051068}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402161}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"} :step/id #uuid "e2be51b6-d1cc-5e3f-8242-1dc23fa98c20" :step/math-academy-id 588702 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222317}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222491 :tutorial/content "So far, we have learned how to find equilibrium points of a system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y).\n$$\n\nHowever, just knowing where equilibrium points are is not enough: we\nalso want to understand *how nearby solutions behave* when we start\nclose to an equilibrium point.\n\nRoughly speaking, there are three qualitatively different behaviors.\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **stable** if for\nany neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ there exists a neighborhood\n$O_1 \\subseteq O$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O_1$ at\nsome time $t = t_0$ remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions which start sufficiently\nclose to the equilibrium remain close to it for all later times,\nalthough they need not converge to it.\n\n tikz \n\n![](images/ca/ca05b3e5d076c74f4d3cf346e0598e99d363a155d41ef10b98d1914602638b76.png)\n\n /tikz \n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **asymptotically\nstable** if it is stable *and* there exists a neighborhood $O$ of\n$\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$\napproaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions which start sufficiently\nclose to the equilibrium remain close to it *and* converge to it as\n$t \\to \\infty.$\n\nNote that asymptotic stability is a stronger condition than stability:\nit adds the requirement that nearby solutions actually reach\n$\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ in the limit.\n\n tikz \n\n![](images/ce/cedd8e777a57901042cb46889650006a9b6e1980dc733d736790e3cc3e3ccfaa.png)\n\n /tikz \n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **unstable** if it\nis not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means there is some neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ from which trajectories can escape, even when started\narbitrarily close to $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ Note that this does not\nrequire trajectories to stay outside $O$ forever; it only requires them to\nleave $O$ at some moment, even if they return later.\n\n tikz \n\n![](images/f7/f7668e3f5ba2b8dc7b4ca3a3e503c55a54a00c2f99648895e052447274bb6d15.png)\n\n /tikz \n\n**Watch out!** These descriptions are *local*: they describe what\nhappens when the initial condition is sufficiently close to the\nequilibrium. They do not require that all trajectories in the entire\nplane behave the same way.\n\nNext, let's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "398c0856-505e-5ecb-b56f-1a57b0837d91" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15932 :tutorial/title "Stability, Asymptotic Stability, and Instability"} :step/id #uuid "2756df62-6611-50b8-b90b-649614f0beca" :step/math-academy-id 655321 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222318}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221413 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221175 :question/id #uuid "e7405495-e774-5508-b22b-e72d82eb4346" :question/math-academy-id "e-21666"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049396}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396867}] :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"} :step/id #uuid "64234359-7fbc-5b3b-b380-92c84702a88e" :step/math-academy-id 588706}] :activity/title "Equilibrium Points and Stability for Systems of ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221469}]}
